1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,883
Break one AI agent, and you learn one thing: it broke.

2
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:11,156
Look closer. This model walks into the same wall eleven times, sure it is one step from the goal.

3
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:15,587
This one wanders into a dead end, and resets far too late.

4
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:20,894
Now break thousands of them, on games none of them has ever seen.

5
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:25,615
The failures stop being noise. They line up into patterns.

6
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:33,905
Each pattern is something agents still can't do. To find them at scale, we built a game factory.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:41,023
The factory writes games from code. Every new seed is a new game, so the supply never runs out.

8
00:00:41,373 --> 00:00:47,457
And difficulty is a dial: easy enough for a small model to learn, hard enough for the best.

9
00:00:47,807 --> 00:00:52,869
The agent sees only pixels: no text, no score, no rule names.

10
00:00:53,219 --> 00:00:55,309
It has six buttons. That's all.

11
00:00:56,217 --> 00:01:01,279
Each new level hides one new rule, which the agent must discover by experimenting.

12
00:01:01,629 --> 00:01:06,737
And a search proves every level solvable, so we always know the best possible run.

13
00:01:07,633 --> 00:01:11,813
We ran frontier models on fresh games, first with our own harness.

14
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:17,999
Then the way labs benchmark them: the full conversation, and maximum reasoning effort.

15
00:01:18,349 --> 00:01:26,058
Run that way, the best model finishes every game, but still needs more moves than the optimal run. Most models finish none.

16
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:34,373
We log every move, and what the model believed. This one is sure that moving right reaches the goal. The screen never changes.

17
00:01:34,723 --> 00:01:38,531
This one stays stuck for a long time before it tries a reset.

18
00:01:39,217 --> 00:01:42,700
This one acts on a belief the screen has already disproved.

19
00:01:43,467 --> 00:01:45,371
Everything they needed was on the screen.

20
00:01:46,267 --> 00:01:49,982
The score rewards solving levels efficiently, and nothing else.

21
00:01:50,332 --> 00:01:57,530
On the judges' own harness, doing nothing scores zero, and stopping after one level earns only that level.

22
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:00,713
Resetting never erases the moves already spent.

23
00:02:01,063 --> 00:02:05,057
And the score is computed in a separate container that the agent cannot reach.

24
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:12,584
Because the supply never runs out, the games are also training data. So can a small model learn from them?

25
00:02:12,934 --> 00:02:25,055
We let a four-billion-parameter model play our games and learn from its own wins. After two rounds, it solves six points more maths problems it never trained on, overtaking an untrained model twice its size.

26
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:35,145
We also built a test outside our games: a rover whose controls are scrambled. It has to work out which button drives which way, find a beacon, and get home.

27
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:40,508
A small four-billion-parameter model completes forty-one missions in a hundred.

28
00:02:40,858 --> 00:02:47,174
After two rounds of training on our games, it completes fifty-four. That gain is statistically significant.

29
00:02:47,967 --> 00:02:54,654
Beyond this hackathon: endless training environments for labs, and private evaluations that can't leak.

30
00:02:55,004 --> 00:02:58,069
Every broken agent shows where AI still has to learn.

31
00:02:58,419 --> 00:02:59,487
Game Factory.

